			<div class="content" id="post-content-4839179">
									

<p>The indigenous American Coyote character escapes to his own metanarrative properties, and the Foundation seeks to stop him from spreading to other folklore.</p>
<p><em>I moved and trimmed your thread summary, since it was taking up extra space in the main forum screen. Try to keep thread descriptions (the text in the little box under the thread title) and titles short so you don't crowd out other authors in the forum listing. ~Zyn</em></p>
<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Mostly an exploration of a cool idea</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Bog-standard SCP format</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> The set of Indigenous American stories about Coyote are an anomalous set of living narrative and metanarrative characteristics, which the Foundation worries might &quot;jump&quot; into other contemporary narratives. &quot;Coyote&quot; fools the Foundation and involves the Chaos Insurgency</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> There is definitely a plot to this, but I hope you'll find it interesting enough to warrant such a big explanation:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>A culture's practices and beliefs determine its stories, right? But what if the stories start to have a mind (or agenda) of their own? A Foundation anthropologist reports that the set of stories about Coyote acts differently from any other set of stories about a character (indigenous or otherwise); specifically, the stories and the overall set itself shares Coyote's characteristics, as well as some instinct for self preservation and an unknown degree of sapience. This worries the Foundation, who are concerned about what would happen if Coyote discovers how to (or maybe just decides to) jump into other contemporary narrative-sets. They have no idea what will happen in that scenario.</p>
<p>A year later, Foundation web-sweeper bots detect a set of movie concepts, novel plots, and fanfiction arcs, many involving birds of some kind, which overall &quot;behaved like a narrative animal, but with more personality&quot;, similarly to the Coyote set. The foundation at first classifies this as a new, similar SCP, but the anthropologist later realizes that it is in fact a containment breach, where &quot;Coyote took the form of a bird&quot;. The site is taken down, and it is discovered that many of the original posts were made by the teenage daughter of a former member of the Chaos Insurgency of Kootenai descent.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The Foundation deals with a metanarrative SCP and gets hoodwinked. The Chaos Insurgency gets involved.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> The most important thing I want to watch out for is that I don't mis-portray parts of any Native American culture(s). That being said, this very much isn't the mythical figure of Coyote as an SCP (that'd be both stupid and impossible to write), but is a conscious set of stories which act similar to the character they portray. The point is to look at how the foundation will handle an SCP like this, and vice versa&#8230;</p>

							</div>

								
